@model JobAndOrganisationViewModel
@*
    Who answers for the post above — the second half of the single "Reports to" row. D31 and its
    addendum.

    IT IS NOT ITS OWN ROW ANY MORE. "Reports to" and "Line manager" were two rows rendered from two
    models and read as two independent facts. They are one: the post above, and whoever answers for
    it. _JobAndOrganisation renders the post and its state; this renders the person.

    FOUR CASES, AND THE SPLIT THAT MATTERS. TopOfLine and NobodyAbove used to be one case called
    None, reading "Nobody — this post answers to no one on the reporting line". True at the top and
    false everywhere else: a Software Engineer under a vacant Manager Technology answers to the
    Director above the gap, and the screen said they answered to no one.

    D28 A STILL DECIDES WHAT MAY BE DERIVED: one holder is a fact and is shown; several is not, and
    nothing picks between them. D31 constrains the list to those holders and requires an answer;
    the addendum adds that when the post above is vacant, "those holders" are the nearest held
    post's — labelled acting, so a manager two levels up never appears unexplained.

    THE FIELD IS STILL LineManagerEmployeeId. No new storage: an acting manager is a person on the
    record, not a second kind of fact.
*@
@{
    var lineManager = Model.LineManager!;
    var editable = Model.Editable;

    // The vacant post's own name, for the acting sentence. A local of the parent partial is not in
    // scope here, so it is read from the model again rather than passed through ViewData.
    var vacantPostName = Model.Position?.ReportsTo.PositionName ?? string.Empty;
}

<div class="mt-1" id="line-manager">
    @if (lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.TopOfLine)
    {
        @* Genuinely nobody above. The row above already says so; nothing more to add here, and a
           second sentence repeating it would be noise. *@
    }
    else if (lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.NobodyAbove)
    {
        @* The post above is empty AND so is everything above it, all the way up. The posts exist
           and are unfilled, which is a different statement from "answers to no one" and is one
           somebody can act on. *@
        @* SLICE F6c: NAMED, NOT COUNTED. "No one above holds a post yet" was true and
           unactionable — a reader could not tell whether one chair was empty or five, or which.
           Naming them turns the sentence into a list of posts somebody can go and fill.

           Composed on the server, like every other list in this product: Arabic's list separator
           is "، " rather than ", ", so even the comma is a resource. *@
        <span class="text-warning" id="line-manager-nobody-above">
            @if (lineManager.VacantAbove.Count > 0)
            {
                @T["No acting manager — {0} are also vacant",
                    string.Join(T[", "].Value, lineManager.VacantAbove)]
            }
            else
            {
                @T["No one above holds a post yet"]
            }
        </span>
    }
    else if (lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.Derived && lineManager.Sole is { } sole)
    {
        @* SAYS NOTHING WHEN THE ROW ABOVE HAS ALREADY SAID IT, and that is the fix rather than an
           omission. With one holder the row reads "Director Technology — Ali Tahir (line manager)":
           the post, and the person who answers for it, which is the whole fact. Printing "Ali
           Tahir" again underneath made one person look like two, and read as though the screen had
           derived the same answer twice from two different places.

           NOT "unless it is acting", WHICH WAS THE FIRST ATTEMPT AND WAS WRONG. This mode also
           covers a person chosen among several holders, and there the row reads "Site Supervisor
           (3 holders)" and names nobody — so going quiet would have hidden the line manager
           altogether. The question is whether the ROW has named them. See
           JobAndOrganisationViewModel.RowAlreadyNamesThePerson. *@
        @if (!Model.RowAlreadyNamesThePerson)
        {
            <span id="line-manager-derived">
                @if (lineManager.IsActing)
                {
                    @* The row says "(vacant)", so this is the only place the acting manager
                       appears — and somebody two levels up appearing unexplained is the defect the
                       first addendum was written for. Named once, with the post they hold. *@
                    @T["{0} — acting ({1})", sole.Name, lineManager.ActingPositionName]
                }
                else
                {
                    @sole.Name
                }
            </span>
        }

        @if (editable)
        {
            @* Carried whether or not anything was printed: the control still has to post what the
               form would save. The hidden field is the control; the sentence was only its label. *@
            <input type="hidden" name="LineManagerEmployeeId" value="@sole.EmployeeId" />
        }
    }
    else if (lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.NeedsChoice)
    {
        @* A CHOICE THAT HAS STOPPED BEING TRUE. Somebody picked this person from the holders of
           the post above, and they no longer hold it. Never silently replaced: picking another
           holder would be the product inventing a line manager, which is what D28 A refuses — so
           the person is named, what changed is said, and the question is asked again. *@
        <span class="text-warning" id="line-manager-needs-choice">
            @T["Needs a new line manager — {0} no longer holds {1}",
                lineManager.StalePersonName, lineManager.PositionName]
        </span>

        @if (editable && lineManager.Holders.Count > 0)
        {
            <select class="form-select form-select-sm mt-1" id="LineManagerEmployeeId"
                    name="LineManagerEmployeeId" data-line-manager-choice required>
                <option value="">@T["Choose…"]</option>
                @foreach (var holder in lineManager.Holders)
                {
                    <option value="@holder.EmployeeId">@holder.Name</option>
                }
            </select>
        }
    }
    else if (lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.Choose)
    {
        @if (editable)
        {
            @* D28 A: several hold the post and nothing picks between them. The list is theirs
               alone — offering the whole tenant here is what D31 replaced. *@
            <select class="form-select form-select-sm mt-1" id="LineManagerEmployeeId"
                    name="LineManagerEmployeeId" data-line-manager-choice required>
                <option value="">@T["Choose…"]</option>
                @foreach (var holder in lineManager.Holders)
                {
                    <option value="@holder.EmployeeId" selected="@(holder.EmployeeId == lineManager.EmployeeId)">
                        @holder.Name
                    </option>
                }
            </select>

            <div class="form-text">
                @if (lineManager.IsActing)
                {
                    @T["{0} is vacant. Choose which holder of {1} is acting.",
                        vacantPostName, lineManager.ActingPositionName]
                }
                else
                {
                    @T["Choose which of them this person answers to."]
                }
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            @* Read-only and nobody chosen yet: the count is already on the row above, so this says
               only that the answer is outstanding. *@
            <span class="text-muted" id="line-manager-unchosen">@T["No line manager chosen"]</span>
        }
    }
    else if (lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.Override && !editable)
    {
        @* THE ONE MODE THAT MUST NAME SOMEBODY HERE. The row above names the post above and
           whoever holds it; an override is by definition not that person, so this is the only
           place they appear. The "Differs from the position's reporting line" label below says
           why, so the two together never read as a contradiction. *@
        <span id="line-manager-override-name">@lineManager.PersonName</span>
    }

    @if (lineManager.DiffersFromTheReportingLine)
    {
        <div class="form-text text-warning" id="line-manager-differs">
            @T["Differs from the position's reporting line."]
        </div>
    }

    @if (editable)
    {
        @* Carried on every mode so a save always says which control produced the value. *@
        <input type="hidden" name="LineManagerOverride" id="line-manager-override"
               value="@(lineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.Override ? "true" : "false")" />

        @if (lineManager.Mode != LineManagerMode.Override)
        {
            @* type="button", NOT submit, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness. This sits
               above the form's real submit, so a second submit button here is the one
               button[type='submit'] finds first — and every caller that pressed "Add employee" or
               "Place" that way silently pressed this instead. It submits through form.submit()
               after setting its flags, which is the same navigation and matches nothing looking
               for a submit control.

               The refresh flag is found by its data attribute rather than by id, because this
               partial is rendered on two forms whose own flags are named differently. *@
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0" id="line-manager-change"
                    onclick="document.getElementById('line-manager-override').value='true';
                             var flag = document.querySelector('[data-refresh-flag]');
                             if (flag) { flag.value = 'true'; }
                             this.form.submit();">@T["Change…"]</button>
        }
    }
</div>
